lesson:
Three tools that make your data say exactly what it means.

**Enums: a fixed set of choices.** If a crop can only be maize, beans, rice or sorghum, don't use Strings (someone will type "Maize " or "maze"). Use an enum:

```
enum Crop {
    MAIZE("Maize", 55), BEANS("Beans", 120);
    private final String label;
    private final int pricePerKg;
    Crop(String label, int pricePerKg) { this.label = label; this.pricePerKg = pricePerKg; }
    double value(double kg) { return kg * pricePerKg; }
}
```

Each constant is a real object with fields and methods. `Crop.values()` lists them all, `Crop.valueOf("BEANS")` turns text into a constant (and throws IllegalArgumentException for unknown text), and `switch` on an enum lets the compiler warn you if you forget a case. Game states (MENU, PLAYING, PAUSED), directions, rarities and traffic lights are all enums.

**Records: data with no boilerplate.** Many classes are just "these few values together". A record writes the constructor, getters, `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString` for you:

```
record Reading(String sensor, double litres, int hour) {
    Reading {   // compact constructor: just the checks
        if (litres < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("litres < 0");
    }
    boolean isNight() { return hour < 6 || hour >= 20; }
}
```

Fields are final, getters are named like the fields (`r.litres()`, not `getLitres()`), two records with the same values are `equals`, and it prints as `Reading[sensor=tank1, litres=40.5, hour=7]`. Use records for values (a reading, a score, a position); use a normal class when the object has changing state (a tank, an account).

**Custom exceptions: errors that explain themselves.** `IllegalArgumentException("bad")` is fine for bad input. But when a *normal business situation* goes wrong, a named exception is clearer, and it can carry details:

```
class OutOfStockException extends Exception {
    private final int available;
    OutOfStockException(String item, int wanted, int available) {
        super("Only " + available + " " + item + " left, wanted " + wanted);
        this.available = available;
    }
    int getAvailable() { return available; }
}
```

**Checked or unchecked?**
- `extends Exception` is **checked**: every method that can throw it must say `throws OutOfStockException`, and callers must catch it or pass it on. Use it for things a caller *should plan for*: stock running out, a file missing.
- `extends RuntimeException` is **unchecked**: no `throws` needed. Use it for programming mistakes: a negative quantity, a null that shouldn't be.

The common mistakes: catching an exception and doing nothing (the problem vanishes and returns as a weirder bug); catching `Exception` everywhere; using Strings where an enum belongs; writing 40 lines of getters when a record would do.

example.code:
enum Light {
    RED(30), GREEN(25), AMBER(5);
    private final int seconds;
    Light(int seconds) { this.seconds = seconds; }
    int seconds() { return seconds; }
    Light next() {
        return switch (this) {
            case RED -> GREEN;
            case GREEN -> AMBER;
            case AMBER -> RED;
        };
    }
}

record Visit(String patient, int day) {
    Visit {
        if (day < 1 || day > 31) throw new IllegalArgumentException("day 1-31");
    }
}

class NoSlotException extends Exception {
    NoSlotException(int day) { super("No free slot on day " + day); }
}

public class Example {
    static Visit book(String patient, int day) throws NoSlotException {
        if (day == 13) throw new NoSlotException(day);
        return new Visit(patient, day);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Light l = Light.RED;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) { System.out.println(l + " for " + l.seconds() + "s"); l = l.next(); }

        try {
            System.out.println(book("Amina", 12));
            System.out.println(book("Juma", 13));
        } catch (NoSlotException e) {
            System.out.println("Sorry: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

